[Response A]
Here's one possible solution:

```
function isValidPrime(num) {
  for (let I = 2; I <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
    if (num % I === 0) {
      return false;
    }
  }
  return num > 1;
}

const nums = [3, 7, 11, 13, 17];
nums.forEach(num => {
  const isPrime = isValidPrime(num);
  if (isPrime) {
    console.log(`${num} is a valid prime number.`);
  } else {
    console.log(`${num} is not a valid prime number.`);
  }
});
```

This function uses a helper `isValidPrime` function to check whether a given number is prime or not. The `isValidPrime` function loops through all odd numbers up to the square root of the input number, checking if the number is divisible by any of them. If so, then it returns `false`, indicating that the number is not prime. Otherwise, it returns `true`.

The main part of the code calls this function with each element of the input array, printing out whether the number is prime or not.
